# PART 11 — GAME ENGINE AND PROGRAMMING ARCHITECTURE

## 31. Which engine should you use?

The two engines I would seriously consider are Godot and Unity.

Both support the kinds of systems required for a top-down or isometric action roguelite.

Godot provides dedicated 2D rendering, physics, animation and navigation features. Unity supports 2D sprites, Tilemaps, physics, animation, lighting and a broader 3D development pipeline. [Godot Engine documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/tutorials/2d/index.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### Godot

Official resources: [Godot Engine documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Godot is worth considering if:
- You want to build a 2D game.
- You prefer a relatively lightweight engine.
- You want an integrated scene and scripting workflow.
- You're comfortable learning GDScript or using C#.

Its scene-based architecture can be useful for organising reusable rooms, enemies and gameplay components.

### Unity

Official resources: [Unity 2D documentation](https://docs.unity.com/en-us/engine/7000.0/manual/unity2d?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Unity is worth considering if:
- You want to work with 3D models and an isometric camera.
- You prefer C#.
- You want access to a large ecosystem of tools and learning materials.
- You may need more advanced 3D rendering or animation workflows.

Unity's official workflow covers fundamentals, scripting, sprites, environment construction, animation, graphics, physics, audio, UI, optimisation and publishing. [Unity Docs](https://docs.unity.com/en-us/engine/6000.6/manual/unity2d/2d-game-development/2d-game-creation-wokflow?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### My recommendation

| Your situation | My recommendation |
|---|---|
| Solo developer, mostly 2D | Start by evaluating Godot |
| Solo developer, 3D models and isometric camera | Evaluate Unity and Godot's 3D capabilities |
| Team already experienced with Unity | Use Unity |
| Team already experienced with Godot | Use Godot |
| Still learning programming | Choose one engine and complete a small project before switching |

There is no universal winner.

The best engine is the one your team can use reliably to build, test and ship the game.

---

## 32. Organise your code into systems

Do not put the entire game into one enormous player script.

Separate the systems according to their responsibilities.

A useful architecture might contain:

**Player controller**
- Movement.
- Dodge.
- Facing direction.
- Input handling.

**Combat system**
- Attack execution.
- Hit detection.
- Damage resolution.
- Hit reactions.

**Ability system**
- Active abilities.
- Cooldowns or resource costs.
- Ability effects.

**Enemy system**
- Enemy state machines.
- Movement and targeting.
- Attack selection.
- Death.

**Run manager**
- Current run state.
- Room progression.
- Rewards.
- Boss progression.

**Generation system**
- Room selection.
- Encounter selection.
- Reward placement.

**Progression system**
- Permanent resources.
- Unlocks.
- Persistent upgrades.

**UI system**
- Health and resource indicators.
- Reward choices.
- Pause menu.
- Run summary.

**Audio and effects system**
- Sound playback.
- Visual effect spawning.
- Combat feedback.

These systems should communicate through clear interfaces.

For example, an enemy shouldn't need to know how the reward menu is implemented in order to produce a reward when it dies.

The run manager can respond to an encounter-completion event and activate the appropriate reward logic.

### Why modular architecture matters

Suppose you later change how damage is calculated.

If every enemy, weapon and ability contains its own unrelated damage formula, you must update all of them.

If they all use a shared damage-resolution system, the change is much easier to maintain.

Modularity reduces duplicated logic and makes balancing safer.

---

## 33. Use data-driven design

This is one of the highest-value architectural decisions you can make.

**Separate the definition of an item from the code that executes it.**

Consider a weapon.

Its data might include:
- Weapon name.
- Base damage.
- Attack range.
- Attack duration.
- Recovery time.
- Resource cost.
- Associated animation.
- Associated sound.
- Special ability identifier.

The combat system reads those values and executes the weapon's behaviour.

You can then change weapon damage without rewriting the controller.

The same principle applies to enemies, Boons, upgrades and rooms.

### Example: an enemy definition

```text
Enemy ID: Heavy_Guardian

Max Health: 100
Movement Speed: 2.0
Detection Range: 8.0

Attack:
    Damage: 20
    Range: 2.5
    Startup: Configurable
    Recovery: Configurable

Rewards:
    Resource Type: Standard
```

The numbers here are illustrative. They are not a recommendation for final balance.

The key is that the enemy's configuration is separate from the reusable systems that move, animate and damage it.

### Data-driven upgrades

You can also define upgrades using structured data.

For example:

```text
Upgrade ID: Marked_Target

Trigger:
    OnHit

Condition:
    TargetHasStatus(Marked)

Effect:
    ApplyBonusDamage

Parameters:
    BonusDamage: Configurable
```

This is pseudocode, not a ready-to-run implementation.

For simple upgrades, a data-driven approach can make the game much easier to balance.

For complex upgrades that introduce entirely new behaviour, you may need custom logic.

A practical system supports both.

