# PART 12 — HOW TO IMPLEMENT THE RUN SYSTEM

## 34. Create a run manager

The run manager coordinates everything that happens during a run.

It should know:
- Which region the player is in.
- Which room is active.
- Which rooms have been completed.
- Which rewards are available.
- Which upgrades the player has acquired.
- Whether the player is alive.
- Whether the boss has been defeated.
- Whether the run has ended.

It should not be responsible for every detail of combat.

Its job is to coordinate the run, not replace the player controller or enemy AI.

### A simple run-state model

```text
RUN_START
    ↓
ROOM_LOADING
    ↓
COMBAT_ACTIVE
    ↓
ENCOUNTER_COMPLETE
    ↓
REWARD_SELECTION
    ↓
NEXT_ROOM
    ↓
BOSS_ENCOUNTER
    ↓
RUN_COMPLETE
```

Defeat can lead to a separate run-ending state.

Each transition should have a clear condition.

For example, the encounter becomes complete when all required enemies have been defeated and any encounter-specific conditions have been satisfied.

The reward screen should not appear simply because the last enemy disappeared if the encounter also requires the player to activate an object or survive a timed phase.

## 35. Separate run data from permanent data

You should maintain two distinct categories of saved information.

### Current-run data

This might include:
- Current health.
- Current Magick or other combat resource.
- Current weapon configuration.
- Temporary upgrades.
- Current region and room.
- Current run rewards.
- Enemies defeated during the run.

### Persistent profile data

This might include:
- Permanently unlocked weapons.
- Permanent upgrades.
- Retained resources.
- Unlocked regions.
- Discovered items.
- Settings and accessibility preferences.

When the run ends, the game resolves the current-run data into the appropriate permanent rewards and clears temporary data.

This prevents a common problem: temporary upgrades accidentally surviving into the next run.

### Saving safely

For a prototype, you can save a small structured profile.

As the game grows, consider:
- Save-file versioning.
- Validation of loaded data.
- Handling missing or outdated fields.
- Safe handling of interrupted saves.
- Separating user settings from gameplay progress.

You do not need a complex cloud-save system for your first prototype.

You do need to avoid losing the player's permanent progress when the game closes unexpectedly.

