# PART 13 — BALANCING AND PLAYTESTING

## 36. Balancing is a process, not a one-time calculation

You cannot reliably balance an action roguelite entirely by looking at spreadsheets.

Mathematical models are useful, but real combat produces interactions that are difficult to predict.

A damage upgrade might appear modest on paper yet become extremely powerful when combined with a rapid weapon and a status effect.

An enemy may seem easy when tested alone but become overwhelming when paired with a ranged attacker.

This is why you need repeated playtesting.

### 36.1 Measure more than win rate

Track:
- How often players complete a run.
- How much health remains after encounters.
- How often players take damage from each enemy.
- How often a particular upgrade is selected.
- How frequently players use each ability.
- Which encounters cause repeated failures.
- Which builds dominate.
- How long players spend in a run.
- Where players abandon a run or stop playing.

These measurements help identify problems, but they don't explain everything.

For example, an upgrade may have a high selection rate because it is powerful, or because its description is easier to understand than the alternatives.

You need to observe players and ask why they made particular decisions.

### 36.2 Use a balance spreadsheet

For every weapon, record:
- Base damage.
- Attack rate.
- Attack range.
- Recovery duration.
- Resource cost.
- Expected damage against a standard enemy.
- Performance against multiple enemies.
- Performance against bosses.

For each enemy, record:
- Health.
- Damage.
- Movement speed.
- Attack range.
- Attack startup.
- Recovery duration.
- Behavioural complexity.

This provides a shared reference for balancing.

### 36.3 Compare damage over time carefully

A weapon with high damage per hit may deal less damage over time than a fast weapon.

A simple theoretical estimate is:

\[
\text{DPS}=
\frac{\text{Damage per hit}}{\text{Time between attacks}}
\]

But this calculation ignores several important factors:
- Missed attacks.
- Time spent repositioning.
- Enemy movement.
- Attack recovery.
- Damage modifiers.
- Invulnerability windows.
- Overkill damage.

For your game, theoretical DPS should be treated as a starting point.

Actual encounter performance matters more.

### 36.4 Test the extremes

For every important system, test:
- The weakest plausible build.
- The strongest plausible build.
- A build that focuses on one mechanic.
- A build that combines several mechanics.
- A player who understands the game well.
- A player who is learning it.

You want the game to remain understandable and enjoyable across different levels of skill.

### 36.5 Build an internal test arena

Create a simple room where you can spawn enemies, change weapons, select upgrades and adjust values.

This will make development significantly faster.

Without a test arena, you may need to start an entire run just to test a change to a weapon.

Your test arena should eventually support:
- Spawning any enemy.
- Changing enemy health.
- Switching weapons.
- Applying upgrades.
- Resetting the encounter.
- Displaying combat statistics.

This is a developer tool, not a feature that needs to appear in the final game.

