# PART 14 — THE MOST IMPORTANT DESIGN MISTAKES TO AVOID

## 37. Building too much before the combat works

This is the biggest risk for your project.

You might want:
- Ten regions.
- Twenty weapons.
- Fifty enemies.
- Several bosses.
- Hundreds of upgrades.
- Elaborate mythology.
- A huge crafting system.
- Multiple progression currencies.

Those ideas are exciting, but they create enormous amounts of work.

If the basic combat is not satisfying, all that additional content will be built on an unstable foundation.

**Do not build a large game first. Build a small version of the game you want to make, then expand it.**

## 38. Making every upgrade increase damage

If most upgrades simply increase damage by a percentage, players may eventually feel that the system offers many choices but few genuinely different strategies.

Use a mixture of direct improvements, behaviour changes, synergies and utility.

Every upgrade doesn't need to be revolutionary, but the overall system should create meaningful decisions.

## 39. Making randomness the source of difficulty

Randomness can create variety, but it should not make the game unfair.

The room generator should not produce encounters that the player's current build cannot reasonably handle.

Reward selection should not consistently force players into useless options.

The game can challenge players without guaranteeing that every run is equally strong.

## 40. Giving bosses enormous health instead of better mechanics

A boss with a huge health bar is not automatically a difficult or interesting boss.

A better encounter tests movement, timing, target selection or resource management.

Longer fights should introduce meaningful changes rather than simply repeating the same attack sequence.

## 41. Making permanent progression too powerful

Permanent upgrades can help new players learn the game, but if they remove too much of the challenge, the combat may eventually become trivial.

You should decide which progression systems improve consistency and which systems fundamentally increase power.

The right balance depends on the experience you want to create.

## 42. Copying Hades too closely

Study its underlying systems, not just its visible features.

For example, you can learn from:
- Its room-based progression.
- Its combination of temporary and permanent upgrades.
- Its weapon differentiation.
- Its enemy telegraphs.
- Its repeated-run structure.
- Its use of a central hub.

But your game's combat, visual language, mechanics and cultural identity should be your own.

A useful test is to remove all the mythology-specific names from your design document.

If the remaining game still reads as almost exactly Hades, you probably need a more distinctive mechanical identity.

## 43. Overcomplicating the first version

A large number of systems creates a large number of interactions to debug and balance.

Start with a few well-developed mechanics.

A game with one excellent weapon, five interesting enemies and one well-designed boss is a better prototype than a game with twenty incomplete weapons and dozens of enemies that all behave similarly.

