# PART 16 — YOUR FIRST PLAYABLE BUILD

If I were directing the project, I would define the first playable version as follows.

## Prototype specification

| System | First version |
|---|---|
| Camera | Fixed elevated camera |
| Player | One playable character |
| Movement | Directional movement with collision |
| Dodge | One evasive action with defined timing |
| Weapons | One melee weapon |
| Player abilities | Basic attack and one special attack |
| Enemies | Chaser, ranged attacker and heavy attacker |
| Bosses | One boss with three or four distinct attacks |
| Rooms | Five handcrafted combat rooms |
| Rewards | Three to five upgrade types |
| Run structure | A short sequence of encounters ending in a boss |
| Persistence | One permanent resource or unlock |
| Art | Placeholder visuals initially, followed by one coherent art pass |
| Audio | Basic attack, hit, dodge and enemy attack sounds |
| Saving | Persistent profile and settings |
| Testing | Internal arena and basic combat statistics |

This is deliberately small.

It is large enough to test the central systems without forcing you to build an entire commercial game before learning whether those systems work.

### What counts as success?

The prototype succeeds if:
- Movement feels responsive.
- Attacks reliably hit the intended targets.
- Enemy attacks are understandable.
- The boss requires learning rather than random luck.
- Rewards create at least a few interesting choices.
- A second run feels meaningfully different from the first.
- You can change values without breaking unrelated systems.

If those conditions are met, you have a foundation worth expanding.

