# PART 17 — HOW TO LEARN FROM THE DEVELOPMENT OF HADES

## 50. Study how the game was actually developed

Supergiant's official development documentary, *Developing Hell*, follows the creation of Hades through its development and Early Access period.

The studio has explained that Early Access allowed it to develop the game alongside player feedback, and that the game's modular structure supported this process. [Supergiant Games](https://www.supergiantgames.com/blog/hades-noclip-bluray/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I strongly recommend studying that development process rather than relying exclusively on gameplay videos.

Official resource: [Developing Hell — Supergiant Games](https://www.supergiantgames.com/blog/hades-noclip-bluray/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Pay particular attention to:
- How the team approached iterative development.
- How gameplay systems were expanded.
- How the game changed during development.
- How art, music and programming supported the same design goals.
- How the team evaluated what to add or change.

### Why this matters

The finished game can make its systems appear inevitable.

But development involves experimentation, rejected ideas, revisions and compromises.

Understanding that process is useful because you will face the same general problems, even if your project is much smaller.

## 51. Study Hades II's development as well

Hades II provides another useful lesson: a successful sequel can expand an established formula while still requiring substantial iteration.

The developers have discussed how player feedback during Early Access influenced the game's systems and how they approached changes to mechanics over time. [GamesRadar+](https://www.gamesradar.com/games/hades/weve-learned-to-love-and-trust-the-process-how-hades-2-built-on-supergiants-early-access-legacy-to-deliver-the-best-roguelike-of-2025/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Official resource: [Hades II development updates and announcements](https://www.supergiantgames.com/blog/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Don't interpret this as a reason to release an unfinished game immediately.

The lesson is that important design decisions should be tested, and developers should be willing to change systems that aren't working.

